summary:
The appellant was a tenant of a building in which the husband had housed the wife resulting in a consent decree which is not a valid one and the respondent was a tenant in the suit premises. The respondent filed a suit for eviction of the appellant for eviction of his son and for eviction of her son and for ejectment of his son on the ground that she was a tenant under section 125(3) of the Indian Penal Code and the appellant was not a tenant under the provisions of section 125(3) ofs. The trial court decreed the suit holding that the appellant was a married tenant and that the respondent was not a tenant. The trial court held that the respondent had no jurisdication to consider whether the appellant was entitled to an allowance. The trial court dismissed the suit holding the appellant guilty of the respondent 's eviction and the appeal was dismissed. The High Court held that the appellant had no jurisdic, and that the appellant could not be evicted from the suit premises. In appeal to this Court, it was contended on behalf of the appellant that the appellant 's case was not a fit case for the High Court to interfere with the order of the trial court and the High Court was not justified in interfering with the order passed by the trial court. Dismissing the appeal, ^ HELD: The appellant 's case is that she was not a minor tenant and that she was entitled to the protection of the provisions of the Act and that the provisions of sections 125(3) and 125(3) of the

Act were not complied with. [935 H; 935 B] The appellant was aa tenant under section

